s=s[:a]+r'''3.1 Put $V(t)=J_0K(t)$ and $m(t)=\int_0^t\|V(s)\|\,ds$. The iterated-integral series for the matrix equation has $n$th term bounded by $m(t)^n/n!$: the scalar product integrand is symmetric in its $n$ variables and the ordered simplex is one of the $n!$ equal regions of their product cube, up to null diagonals. Thus the series converges uniformly, with sum bounded by $e^{m(T)}$. It satisfies $S(t)=I+\int_0^tV(s)S(s)\,ds$, since the series under that integral is dominated by $\|V(s)\|e^{m(T)}$. By [F5], $S$ is absolutely continuous and $\dot S=VS$ almost everywhere. Product differentiation gives $(S^TJ_0S)'=0$ almost everywhere, so [F5] and its initial value give symplecticity at every time. A finite subdivision, using uniform continuity and invertibility of $S$, makes each $S(t)S(t_j)^{-1}$ lie in the neighborhood of step 1.1. Set $U(t)=L(S(t)S(t_j)^{-1})U(t_j)$, with $U(0)=I$. To justify the Schwartz calculus, restrict each parameter path to a small compact neighborhood where the local factors are smooth. The seminorm estimates of step 1.1, also for their parameter derivatives, imply $p((L(\alpha)-L(\beta))f)\le C_{p,q}|\alpha-\beta|q(f)$ there and $p(\partial_t L(\alpha(t))f)\le C_{p,q}|\alpha'(t)|q(f)$ almost everywhere, for some Schwartz seminorm $q$. One can obtain the first bound by integrating along the straight parameter segment inside that neighborhood; finite smaller neighborhoods suffice. Matrix inversion and the local factor parameters have bounded derivatives on those sets, and $|S'|\le e^{m(T)}\|V\|$ is integrable. These bounds prove absolute continuity in every seminorm, the chain and product rules almost everywhere, and the same assertions for the inverse factors. Step 2.1 therefore gives $i\hbar\dot U=H_KU$ almost everywhere, with continuous matching at joins and covariance from step 1.1. If two such evolutions have the same initial vector, their difference $w(t)$ is absolutely continuous in $L^2$ and satisfies the same equation. Its squared norm is absolutely continuous and has derivative $2\operatorname{Re}\langle-iH_Kw/\hbar,w\rangle=0$ almost everywhere, because the real symmetrically ordered expression is symmetric on Schwartz space by integration by parts. By [F5] the difference remains zero. Density and unitarity extend uniqueness to all $L^2$. Thus existence, uniqueness, inverse continuity and covariance hold for the full integrable coefficient class in the Statement. [F1, F4, F5, step 1.1, step 2.1, construct, algebra]'''+s[b:]
s=s.replace('defining $c$ by the exponential of the integral of this continuous, piecewise-smooth coefficient.','defining $c$ by the exponential of the indefinite integral of this $L^1$ coefficient; it is integrable because $b,d$ are integrable and $Z$ is continuous and bounded on the compact time interval. By [F5] and the ordinary smooth chain rule on the bounded range of this integral, $c$ is absolutely continuous and satisfies that equation almost everywhere.')
s=s.replace('makes $g$ Schwartz, smoothly in time on each piece; compact time sets have a uniform positive lower eigenvalue, so all required Schwartz and $L^2$ differentiations are justified by polynomial times Gaussian bounds.','makes $g$ Schwartz. On the compact time interval $\\operatorname{Im}Z$ has a uniform positive lower eigenvalue. Every parameter derivative of this Gaussian is a polynomial times a Gaussian, so the same seminorm estimates as in step 3.1 make $g$ absolutely continuous in each Schwartz seminorm and justify the almost-everywhere derivatives below.')
